.Dd November 11, 2026
.Dt SHUTDOWN 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm shutdown
.Nd shut down part of a full-duplex connection
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/socket.h
.Ft int
.Fn shutdown "int fd" "int how"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn shutdown
operates on a TCP socket, connected, connecting or listening, and on a
UDP or ICMP datagram socket, judging each as Linux's inet_shutdown does.
The socket keeps its connection until its close, which finishes the
connection's close.
.Pp
The
.Fa how
argument selects a direction:
.Bl -tag -width SHUT_RDWR
.It Dv SHUT_RD
Value 0.
Close the receiving half, as Linux does.
A read returns the bytes received and then, with none left, end of input
at once, as does one another thread is blocked in;
.Xr poll 2
reports
.Dv POLLIN
and
.Dv POLLRDHUP .
The peer's data is still taken while this side's sending half is open,
and a read returns it, but the window it may fill is no longer
announced as the reader drains it.
Once this side's FIN is sent or queued and before the peer's FIN, data
past what was received ends the connection with a reset, counted in
TCPAbortOnData, as RFC 1122, section 4.2.2.13, asks.
.It Dv SHUT_WR
Value 1.
Close the sending half, as Linux does.
In the established or close-wait state, queued output is sent and then
the FIN, so the peer receives end of input after those bytes, and a
later send fails with
.Er EPIPE ,
as does one another thread is blocked in, waiting for room;
.Xr poll 2
reports
.Dv POLLOUT ,
since a send does not wait.
The peer's data and its FIN still reach a read, which is how a client
such as
.Xr nc 1
sends its request and reads the answer.
In a later closing state, whose FIN is on its way already, nothing
else changes.
In the second handshake state of a simultaneous open,
.Dv SYN_RCVD ,
no FIN is sent, then or when the handshake ends, as on Linux, and a
send waits for the handshake and then fails with
.Er EPIPE .
.It Dv SHUT_RDWR
Value 2.
Both: the FIN goes after the queued output, a read returns the bytes
received and then end of input, and data the peer sends past what was
received ends the connection with a reset, counted in TCPAbortOnData,
after which a read or send reports
.Er ECONNRESET .
.Xr poll 2
reports
.Dv POLLHUP
too.
.El
.Pp
A socket whose connection is still in its first handshake state,
.Dv SYN_SENT ,
is disconnected instead, whatever
.Fa how
is, as on Linux: the attempt ends with nothing sent, a
.Xr connect 2
blocked on it returns
.Er ECONNRESET ,
which is otherwise the socket's pending error, a local port the attempt
chose is given back, and the socket may connect again.
.Pp
A listener shut with
.Dv SHUT_RD
or
.Dv SHUT_RDWR
stops listening, as on Linux: each connection waiting to be accepted is
ended with a reset, so its peer reads
.Er ECONNRESET ,
the handshakes in progress are dropped, an
.Xr accept 2
blocked on the socket returns
.Er EINVAL ,
and the socket may listen again.
.Dv SHUT_WR
alone leaves a listener as it is.
.Pp
A TCP socket whose connection has ended, by a reset, by a timeout or
in the time-wait state, takes the halves but returns
.Er ENOTCONN ,
as Linux's closed socket does, and sends nothing.
.Pp
A UDP or ICMP datagram socket records the halves shut, and every
waiter is woken.
With the receiving half shut, a blocking receive returns the
datagrams still queued and then 0, with no sender, where one that does
not wait fails with
.Er EAGAIN ,
as on Linux, and
.Xr poll 2
reports
.Dv POLLIN
and
.Dv POLLRDHUP ,
with both halves shut
.Dv POLLHUP
too.
Datagrams are still taken, and a send still goes.
A socket with no peer set by
.Xr connect 2
returns
.Er ENOTCONN
after taking the halves, as Linux does, so that a thread blocked in its
receive can still be stopped.
.Pp
When the socket is closed with received data still unread, the
connection ends with a reset in place of the FIN, counted in
TCPAbortOnClose, whatever
.Fn shutdown
did before; see
.Xr tcp 7 .
.Pp
This operation is subcall 13 of i386
.Xr socketcall 2 ,
system call 102.
.Pp
On x86-64
.Nm shutdown
is call 48.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn shutdown
returns zero on success.
On failure it returns -1 and sets
.Va errno .
The kernel interface returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/musl/sockflagmusl.c
sends a request over the loopback, shuts the client's sending half with
.Dv SHUT_WR ,
checks that a send then fails with
.Er EPIPE ,
and reads the server's answer, which the server sends only after it has
read the request and its end.
.Pa tests/loopprobe.c
shuts a client whose peer has closed with
.Dv SHUT_WR
and checks that a blocking and a nonblocking read return zero.
The host tests
.Pa tests/host/tcp_reset_hosttest.c
and
.Pa tests/host/socket_wake_hosttest.c
check a listener's shutdown, the states judged, a read and a send after
a reset and a datagram socket's receive and poll.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EOPNOTSUPP]"
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fa fd
is not an open descriptor.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
The
.Fn socketcall
argument array cannot be read.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa how
is not 0, 1, or 2.
.It Bq Er ENOTCONN
The socket is a stream that never connected or whose connection has
ended, or a datagram socket with no peer; the last two still take the
halves.
.It Bq Er ENOTSOCK
.Fa fd
does not refer to a socket.
.It Bq Er EOPNOTSUPP
The socket is a raw, packet or netlink socket, as Linux's
sock_no_shutdown answers.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr connect 2 ,
.Xr poll 2 ,
.Xr recv 2 ,
.Xr send 2 ,
.Xr socket 2 ,
.Xr socketcall 2 ,
.Xr tcp 7
